(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pd1-exam3-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Shoulder abduction by the deltoid is primarily innervated by which nerve roots?","choices":["L2 and L3 (femoral nerve)","S1 alone (tibial nerve)","C5 and C6 (axillary nerve)","T8 and T9 (intercostal nerves)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Shoulder abduction by the deltoid is primarily innervated by the C5 and C6 nerve roots through the axillary nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is the uterus palpated bimanually?","choices":["Only the pelvic hand is used inside the vagina, with no abdominal hand pressing down externally at all","The uterus cannot reliably be palpated on bimanual exam in any patient regardless of body habitus","Only the abdominal hand presses down externally, with no vaginal finger insertion performed at any point","Free hand on the abdomen presses down while the pelvic hand elevates the cervix, \"grasping\" the uterus between the two hands"],"answer":3,"exp":"This bimanual technique — abdominal hand pressing down, pelvic hand elevating the cervix — allows the uterus to be grasped between the two hands. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of pubarche, what characterizes Stage 1?","choices":["Hair adult in type but covering a smaller area, with no spread to the medial thighs","Sparse growth of long, pigmented hair at the base of the labia","Prepubertal, with vellus hair similar to the abdominal wall","Adult in type and quantity, with horizontal distribution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stage 1 pubarche is prepubertal, showing only fine vellus hair similar to that on the abdominal wall. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Before evaluating the thoracic and lumbar spine, the examiner inspects the pelvis for symmetry of which landmarks?","choices":["The iliac crests and posterior superior iliac spines","The acromion processes and clavicles","The medial malleoli and heels","The mastoid processes and tragus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvis is inspected for symmetry in the level of the iliac crests and the posterior superior iliac spines, since the pelvis is the base of the spine. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the fundus of the uterus?","choices":["The distal, fan-like end of the fallopian tube","The intravaginal, visible portion of the cervix","The domed superior area of the uterine body","The retrouterine peritoneal pouch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fundus is the domed superior area of the uterine body. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Beyond naming the allergen, what does the lecture require be documented for allergies?","choices":["The date the allergy was first diagnosed by a clinician","The reaction and its severity","Whether a substitute medication was tolerated afterwards","The result of any formal allergy testing performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires the reaction and severity, covering medication, food, and environmental allergies, and says to verify at every appointment. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The lecture summarises the subjective section as which of the following?","choices":["The measurements you recorded during the encounter","The conclusions you reached after examining the patient","The orders you intend to place before the patient leaves","What the patient said"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture subtitles the subjective section “what the patient said” and describes it as a narrative. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What three pressure levels should be applied during systematic breast palpation?","choices":["Pressure level is not standardized","Light, medium, and deep pressure","Only light pressure, to avoid causing discomfort","Only deep pressure, to reach the chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systematic breast palpation applies light, medium, and deep pressure at each point. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"Normal finger flexion, making a fist, is approximately how many degrees at the joints?","choices":["About 90 degrees","About 20 degrees","About 45 degrees","About 160 degrees"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal finger flexion, making a fist with the thumb across the knuckles, is approximately 90 degrees, with finger extension about 30 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"Hip flexion by the iliopsoas is primarily innervated by which nerve roots?","choices":["L2, L3, and L4 (femoral nerve)","C5 and C6 (axillary nerve)","S1 and S2 (sciatic nerve)","C7 and C8 (radial nerve)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hip flexion by the iliopsoas is primarily innervated by the L2, L3, and L4 nerve roots through the femoral nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"Besides constricting the pupil, the oculomotor nerve elevates the upper eyelid by innervating which muscle?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi muscle","The superior oblique muscle","The levator palpebrae superioris","The lateral rectus muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve elevates the upper eyelid by innervating the levator palpebrae superioris, in addition to constricting the pupil and moving most eye muscles. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Normal ankle dorsiflexion is approximately what range?","choices":["About 35 to 50 degrees","About 70 to 90 degrees","About 110 degrees","About 10 to 20 degrees"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal ankle dorsiflexion is approximately 10 to 20 degrees, while plantar flexion is about 35 to 50 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Muscle tone is evaluated by assessing what?","choices":["The muscle's size and contour on inspection","The muscle's resistance to passive stretch","The strength of the muscle against resistance","The reflex response to a tendon tap"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle tone is the slight residual tension in a muscle, evaluated by assessing its resistance to passive stretch. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does the lecture's KISS principle stand for, and what does it ask of a note?","choices":["Keep Information Sequential and Signed — chronological order only","Keep It Structured and Standardised — identical wording each visit","Keep It Simple Students — logical progression, headings, specificity","Keep It Short and Systematic — one page for each encounter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs it with head-to-toe progression, appropriate headings, being specific, and avoiding redundancy. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How should the differential diagnoses be ordered and justified?","choices":["Alphabetically, with testing listed separately in the plan","Most likely first, with evidence from the history and examination explaining why","Least likely first, so that the most serious possibility appears at the end of the list","In the order the symptoms were reported by the patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for three or more, the first being most likely and justified from history and physical findings, with the others explained as considered but less likely. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why should each step of the pelvic exam be explained to the patient before proceeding?","choices":["It is part of the general approach to reduce patient anxiety and build trust during a sensitive exam","It is a legal requirement mandated by the state medical board, with no additional clinical benefit","It replaces the need for draping, since verbal explanation alone maintains adequate patient privacy","It is only necessary if a chaperone is not present in the room during the examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Explaining each step before proceeding, along with smooth movements, is part of the general technique to keep the patient informed and comfortable. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise the patient education element of the plan?","choices":["Detailed","Optional when the patient is already familiar with the condition","Limited to a single sentence for readability","Replaced by a printed handout in most encounters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture writes patient education in the plan as DETAILED, and its worked example runs to several specific instructions covering expected timeline, side effects, fluids, hygiene, and when to call. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a barrier to communication?","choices":["Documenting the encounter after the patient has left","Ordering diagnostic testing before the history is complete","Using a standardized rather than a real patient","Cultural differences between the clinician and the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists emotional, sensory, cultural, disability and other health problems, language, and terminology as barriers. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which bones form the ankle region?","choices":["The femur, patella, and tibia","The tibia, fibula, and talus","The radius, ulna, and carpals","The humerus, scapula, and clavicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ankle is formed by the tibia, fibula, and talus, articulating at the tibiotalar and subtalar joints. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which category does the lecture list among the things to consider when judging pertinence?","choices":["The patient's preferred pharmacy and current insurance coverage","The length of the appointment slot the clinic has available","Information that helps rule in or rule out differential diagnoses","Whether the finding was mentioned at any previous clinic visit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists this alongside relation to the chief complaint, prior or related episodes, and risk factors. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Is nipple inversion always abnormal?","choices":["Yes, inversion always requires biopsy","Yes, inversion always indicates malignancy","No, inverted is a normal variant","Nipple position has no clinical significance at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nipple inversion can be a normal anatomic variant, whereas the nipple is usually everted. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why should the drape be depressed at the midline during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It is not a recommended technique in modern examination practice","To improve the overhead lighting angle for the exam","To allow eye contact between the patient and examiner","To keep the sterile instrument tray free of contamination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Depressing the drape's midline allows the patient and examiner to maintain eye contact, aiding relaxation and communication. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Position sense (proprioception) and vibration are carried primarily by which pathway?","choices":["The spinothalamic tracts","The corticospinal tracts","The posterior columns","The spinocerebellar tracts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Position sense and vibration are carried primarily by the posterior columns. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"When testing the olfactory nerve, which type of substance should be avoided?","choices":["Pleasant familiar aromas like coffee","Any substance the patient recognizes","Substances tested with eyes closed","Irritating substances such as menthol or ammonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"When testing smell, irritating substances such as menthol or ammonia should be avoided; a pleasant familiar aroma like coffee or cinnamon is preferred. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"A hinge joint, such as the elbow or interphalangeal joints, permits what motion?","choices":["Wide-ranging motion in every plane","Motion in one plane — flexion and extension","No appreciable movement at all","Only slight gliding movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"A hinge joint permits motion in one plane, flexion and extension, as in the elbow and the interphalangeal joints of the hand and foot. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"During the rectovaginal exam, which finger is introduced into the vagina and which into the anus?","choices":["Only the index finger is used, entering both orifices sequentially","Index finger into the vagina, middle finger into the anus","Middle finger into the vagina, index finger into the anus","Both index and middle fingers into the vagina only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The index finger is placed in the vagina while the middle finger is introduced into the anus for the rectovaginal exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"The nucleus pulposus, which cushions bony movement, is found within what?","choices":["The synovial cavity of a ball-and-socket joint","A fluid-filled bursa over a tendon","The joint capsule of a hinge joint","The fibrocartilaginous discs of cartilaginous joints"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nucleus pulposus is contained within the fibrocartilaginous discs of cartilaginous joints and cushions bony movement. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"A patient describes her chest discomfort as “like something heavy sitting on me.” Which of the seven attributes of a symptom has she just given you?","choices":["Timing","Associated manifestations","Quality","Quantity or severity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Quality is the character of the symptom in the patient's own terms, such as sharp, dull, crushing or burning. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the fibrous tissue of the breast responsible for?","choices":["Providing the primary blood supply to the tissue","Providing supportive structure for the glandular tissue","Producing milk for the infant during lactation","Draining lymphatic fluid from the glandular tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fibrous tissue, including Cooper's ligament, supports the glandular tissue of the breast. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which fibrous bands connect bone to bone?","choices":["Tendons","Bursae","Cartilage","Ligaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ligaments are ropelike bundles of fibrous tissue that connect bone to bone. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"What guidance does the lecture give when you are unsure how far the examination should extend?","choices":["Examine every system in full, since omissions cannot be corrected later","Limit the examination strictly to the system named in the chief complaint","Defer the examination until the diagnostic testing has returned","Go a system up and down from the affected system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this as the rule when in doubt about how wide to examine. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A rectal examination is not performed because the patient declines. How does the lecture say this should appear in the note?","choices":["The section should be left blank so no claim is made","The section should be marked normal since nothing abnormal was seen","The section should be documented as not done, with an explanation","The section should be deleted entirely from the template"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly this format, with examples such as patient refused, deferred by preceptor, or deferred per assignment instructions. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the lecture require of the first diagnosis listed in the differential?","choices":["It should be the most likely, explained using the history and examination","It should be the most dangerous one, whatever its actual likelihood","It should be the one the patient is most concerned about having","It should be the one that is cheapest to exclude with further testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for the most likely diagnosis first, justified from the history and examination findings. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which joint type is freely movable, with bones covered by articular cartilage and separated by a fluid-filled cavity?","choices":["A fibrous joint","A cartilaginous joint","A synovial joint","A juxta-articular joint"],"answer":2,"exp":"A synovial joint is freely movable, its bones covered by articular cartilage and separated by a synovial cavity whose membrane secretes lubricating synovial fluid. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of breast development, what characterizes Stage 4?","choices":["Areola and nipple project to form a secondary mound above the breast","Breast bud stage with a round mound and increased areola diameter","Preadolescent stage, with elevation of only the nipple and no breast bud","Further enlargement with areola and breast still confluent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Stage 4 is characterized by the areola and nipple projecting to form a secondary mound above the level of the breast. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which axillary node group drains most of the breast tissue, located at the lower border of pectoralis major inside the axillary fold?","choices":["Infraclavicular nodes","Pectoral (anterior) nodes","Central nodes","Subscapular (posterior) nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pectoral (anterior) nodes, at the lower border of pectoralis major inside the axillary fold, drain most of the breast tissue. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the order in which information is obtained versus documented?","choices":["Information is not always obtained in the order it needs to be documented","Information must be obtained in exactly the order it will be written","The note should be written in the order the patient volunteered facts","Documentation order is left entirely to individual preference"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes documentation has guidelines for organizing information while the interview is more freeform, usually moving from general to more specific. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which joints does the ankle region include?","choices":["The tibiotalar joint and the subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint","The glenohumeral and acromioclavicular joints","The radiocarpal and midcarpal joints","The atlanto-occipital and atlantoaxial joints"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ankle includes the tibiotalar joint and the subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"The patellar (quadriceps) deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve roots?","choices":["L2, L3, and L4","C5 and C6","C6 and C7","S1 only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The patellar, or quadriceps, deep tendon reflex tests primarily the L2, L3, and L4 nerve roots. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"What forms at the areola where each glandular duct opens, and what is its function during lactation?","choices":["A sebaceous gland that secretes an oily lubricant onto the areola","A dilated duct opening that accumulates milk during lactation","A cluster of lymph nodes draining the axillary lymphatics","A fibrous attachment point anchoring Cooper's ligaments to the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"At the areola, each duct dilates where it opens, accumulating milk during lactation. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"The spinal accessory nerve (cranial nerve eleven) is tested by which maneuver?","choices":["Raising the eyebrows and smiling widely","Shrugging the shoulders and turning the head against resistance","Protruding the tongue and moving it sideways","Clenching the teeth and puffing the cheeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spinal accessory nerve is tested by having the patient shrug the shoulders (trapezius) and turn the head (sternocleidomastoid) against resistance. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which element closes the LOCQSMAT mnemonic?","choices":["Treatment","Timing","Temporal pattern","Tenderness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. LOCQSMAT ends with treatment, after additional symptoms. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where does the vagina lie anatomically?","choices":["Lateral to the labia majora","Superior to the uterine fundus","Between the ovaries and fallopian tubes","Between the bladder and the rectum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vagina is a hollow muscular tube lying between the bladder and the rectum. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which list matches the seven attributes of a symptom as this lecture gives them?","choices":["Site, spread of the pain, character, severity, duration, frequency, and precipitating cause","Onset, duration, severity, frequency, laterality, radiation, and the factors giving relief","Location, quality, quantity, timing, onset, palliative or provocative factors, associated manifestations","Position, pattern, precipitant, progression, prior episodes, pain score, and management plan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These are the seven attributes named on the subjective slide. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"During the axillary lymph node examination, what characteristics are assessed for each palpable node?","choices":["Only node location, not its physical characteristics","Only whether a node is present or absent","Only node color, visualized through the skin","Size, consistency, mobility, and tenderness"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific characteristics assessed for axillary lymph nodes on palpation. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"A student writes “Lungs: normal.” under the objective section. What does the lecture say is wrong with this?","choices":["The word normal is an abbreviation and should be spelled out","It never says what normal means for this particular patient","Lung findings belong in the assessment rather than the objective section","Nothing at all, provided the same wording is used consistently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks you to be specific, since normal and abnormal alone do not convey the finding. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which is one of the six categories of the neurological examination?","choices":["Cranial nerves","Blood pressure","Heart auscultation","Abdominal percussion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The six categories of the neuro exam are mental status, cranial nerves, motor system, reflexes, sensory system, and coordination/station/gait. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the vestibule?","choices":["A fossa that lies medial to the labia minora","The cutaneous covering of the clitoris","The area between the introitus and the anus","The membrane covering the introitus in a virginal female"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vestibule is the fossa medial to the labia minora, containing the urethral meatus and vaginal introitus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which item does the lecture place in the past medical history?","choices":["Alcohol, nicotine and recreational drug use","The three-generation account of relatives' health","Occupation, travel, and support systems","Age-appropriate screenings and their results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists major illnesses, surgeries, hospitalizations, childhood illnesses, immunizations, obstetric, gynecologic or genitourinary history where applicable, and age-appropriate screenings with results, all with specific dates and timelines. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"At what angle and along which wall is the closed speculum introduced?","choices":["No specific angle matters at all, any insertion approach works equally well","Downward angle of 45 degrees, along the posterior wall, then vertical","Upward angle of 45 degrees, along the anterior vaginal wall","Straight horizontal insertion from the very first moment of contact"],"answer":1,"exp":"The closed speculum is introduced at a 45-degree downward angle along the posterior vaginal wall, oriented vertically. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"What general appearance findings are assessed on breast inspection, besides color and nipple changes?","choices":["Contour, masses, dimpling, and retraction","Only the pattern of axillary hair growth","Only the symmetry of the underlying rib cage","Only hair distribution on the chest wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are additional general appearance findings assessed on breast inspection, alongside color, thickening, size, and symmetry. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the pain scale in the history of present illness?","choices":["A score above seven automatically requires immediate escalation","Record the score and ask what a ten means for that patient","The scale should be replaced with a description in the patient's words","The score should be recorded only if the complaint is pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs the one-to-ten pain scale with the question of what a ten means for the patient, because the anchor differs between people. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"For a patient with breast implants performing self-examination, what specific technique is recommended?","choices":["Press firmly inward at the edge of the implants to feel the ribs beneath, checking carefully for unusual lumps or bumps","Palpation should avoid the area directly over the implant entirely, examining only the surrounding tissue","Implants make self-examination unnecessary, since imaging alone detects any underlying abnormality","Only light, superficial pressure should ever be used near the implant edges, to avoid rupturing the shell"],"answer":0,"exp":"This technique helps detect lumps that might otherwise be obscured by the implant by pressing at the implant edges to feel the underlying ribs. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which single eye muscle is innervated by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four)?","choices":["The lateral rectus","The medial rectus","The superior oblique","The inferior rectus"],"answer":2,"exp":"By the mnemonic SO4, the superior oblique is innervated by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four). — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"How many quadrants is the breast divided into for surface anatomy description?","choices":["4 quadrants","8 quadrants","2 quadrants","6 quadrants"],"answer":0,"exp":"The breast is divided into 4 quadrants for surface anatomy description. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"At what level are the ovaries suspended?","choices":["At the level of the introitus","At the level of the symphysis pubis","At the level of the umbilicus","ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The small, almond-shaped ovaries are suspended from the oviducts and uterus at the level of the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine). — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which element does the lecture include in the identifying data at the top of a history and physical?","choices":["The working diagnosis at the time of arrival","The referring clinician's contact details","Reliability of the source of the history","A summary of the most recent laboratory results"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists name, sex, birth date, age, race or ethnic origin, source of history, reliability of that source, and the date and time of the history. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"During inspection of the musculoskeletal system, the examiner primarily does what?","choices":["Uses the eyes to observe posture, symmetry, and deformities","Uses the hands to feel for tenderness, warmth, and swelling","Moves each joint through its range of motion","Strikes tendons to elicit reflexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Inspection means using the eyes to observe the patient's general posture, body symmetry, and any apparent deformities, curvature, muscle wasting, or swelling. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which step does the lecture include among its suggested approach to developing differentials?","choices":["Choose the most probable diagnosis first, then collect the findings that support it","Defer all hypotheses until every laboratory result has returned","Restrict consideration to problems within a single organ system","Identify abnormal findings, consider their anatomic location, and look for patterns"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists identifying abnormal findings, considering anatomic location, looking for patterns, seeking probable cause, generating hypotheses, and then testing them. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Besides the final diagnosis, what else does the lecture include in the assessment?","choices":["Only those conditions newly diagnosed at this particular visit","Other conditions requiring assessment or treatment, chronic and concurrent","Only those conditions that will be treated with medication","Only those conditions the patient raised in the chief complaint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists chronic conditions and concurrent diagnoses alongside the final diagnosis. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
